<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

  <!--
    Android 16 will not promote a notification into the status-bar chip (or
    into whatever an OEM builds on top of it, such as Samsung's Now Bar) unless
    the app holds this, no matter how correctly the notification is built: the
    system checks the POST_PROMOTED_NOTIFICATIONS app op and declines the
    promotion silently, leaving an ordinary notification in the shade with
    nothing logged to explain it.

    Declared here rather than in the host app so installing the package is
    enough — manifest merging carries it into the app. It is a normal,
    install-time permission with no runtime prompt; the user can still revoke
    it per app in Settings, which is what canPostPromotedNotifications()
    reports and what getCapabilities() surfaces as promotedSurface.available.

    Unknown to devices below API 36, which ignore it.
  -->
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_PROMOTED_NOTIFICATIONS" />

  <!--
    POST_NOTIFICATIONS is deliberately NOT declared: it carries a runtime
    prompt, and the decision of when to ask a user for it belongs to the app,
    not to a library it happens to depend on.

    No foreground-service permission either. A live update is a display, not a
    background task — nothing here needs a service to stay running, and
    declaring one would make every app installing this package answer for it
    at review.

    No location permission, for the same reason: this package renders state the
    app gives it and never sources any itself.
  -->

  <application>
    <!--
      Told when the user swipes a live update away, so an activity started with
      `persistent: true` can be posted again.

      Not exported: the only thing that ever triggers this is a PendingIntent
      this package handed to the system, and a dismissal is internal
      bookkeeping. Nothing outside the app has a reason to fire it, and an
      exported receiver would let anything on the device try.
    -->
    <receiver
      android:name="com.liveupdate.DismissReceiver"
      android:exported="false" />

    <!--
      Handles an action marked `endsActivity`, so the button finishes the live
      update in place instead of opening the app to do it. Not exported, for
      the same reason as the one above: the only thing that fires it is a
      PendingIntent this package handed to the system.
    -->
    <receiver
      android:name="com.liveupdate.ActionReceiver"
      android:exported="false" />
  </application>

</manifest>
